'use strict';
// ============================================================================
// STARFALL PROTOCOL — lore: universe codex, timeline, factions, places,
// characters, Anchorage station modules, Archive records and per-card lore.
// Pure data, no logic. Canon reference: docs/story-bible.md.
// archive[].unlock = Archive restoration level (1-12) needed to read a record.
// ============================================================================

const LORE = {
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ universe
  universe: {
    title: "The Reach, 2291",
    intro: "Humanity holds a few hundred worlds strung along the warp lanes, a network of ancient corridors that nobody built and nobody fully understands. Seventy-two years ago every lane failed at once and the sky over a dozen colonies filled with falling ships. People still call that night the Starfall. Now something at the galactic core is singing again, and the machines, the Swarm and the psionics are all listening.",
    sections: [
      { id: "reach", h: "The Reach",
        body: "Spacers call settled space the Reach: about four hundred inhabited worlds, moons and stations spread along one arm of the galaxy and joined by warp lanes. Ships, cargo, news and wars all move along the lanes, and anything off them might as well not exist. Life is crowded and regulated in the core worlds of the Compact and thin, hard and armed everywhere else. Since the Starfall the Reach has been shrinking. Lanes have been lost, colonies cut off, and outposts left to whoever wants them." },
      { id: "compact", h: "The Compact and the Fleet",
        body: "The Colonial Compact is, on paper, the government of most human worlds. It is a charter signed at Concord in 2104 that traded local independence for lane access and protection. Its armed service is the Colonial Fleet, which polices the lanes, runs the Academy and fights whatever the Compact cannot negotiate with. The Fleet has been underfunded for half a century. It has fewer ships than commitments, fewer crews than ships, and a habit of handing junior officers jobs that should belong to admirals." },
      { id: "lanes", h: "Warp Lanes and the Lamps",
        body: "Faster-than-light travel in the Reach means the warp lanes: narrow corridors of folded space between stars. Each lane is anchored at both ends by a Lamp, a dark structure a kilometre across that hums on a radio band of its own. Ships enter a lane at one Lamp and leave it at the next, days later. Humans found the first Lamp at the edge of Sol in 2088 and have followed the lanes ever since. Nobody has built a Lamp and nobody has moved one. Every survey that traces the network far enough reports the same thing: all the lanes eventually run toward the galactic core." },
      { id: "starfall", h: "The Starfall",
        body: "At 04:17 Concord time on 14 March 2219, every Lamp in the Reach flared at once and went dark for nineteen hours. Ships in transit were thrown out of their lanes wherever they happened to be. Some came out in empty space and starved. Some came out inside gravity wells. Over the colony of New Lagos, eleven hundred hulls burned through the atmosphere in one night, and the name stuck. The official inquiry, the Coreward Lane Cascade Report, counted 3,112 ships and 1.4 million dead and listed the cause as undetermined, origin coreward. When the Lamps came back, the Fleet sent demolition crews to kill every Lamp beyond the Forge and the one at the Belt Junction. It never said why." },
      { id: "belt", h: "The Scrap Belt",
        body: "The Scrap Belt is what remains of the Belt Junction, once the busiest lane crossing outside the core worlds. During the Starfall more than four hundred ships were dumped out of their lanes there in under an hour, many of them into each other. Seventy years of drift have ground the wreckage into a loose ring of hulls, containers and ice around the dead husk of Lamp 9. The Fleet held the Belt until the Withdrawal of 2254, then left it to salvagers, smugglers and the raider crews that grew out of them. Everything in the Belt is salvaged from something else, including most of the people." },
      { id: "swarm", h: "The Swarm",
        body: "The Swarm began with one organism, found dormant in 2196 in a sealed chamber beneath the ice of Kesh, a moon inside what is now the Hive Nebula. The chamber walls were Lamp material. Xenobiologists called the organism the Progenitor. It woke, it budded, and within a year Kesh was a nest. The Swarm is less a species than a living library of body plans, each brood bred for a job. Its queens steer it by scent and by something like song. The Swarm Wars of 2201 to 2214 ended in quarantine, not victory. This year, for no reason the Fleet can explain, the Swarm's breeding rate tripled." },
      { id: "forge", h: "The Forge",
        body: "The Forge Worlds are three airless planets in the Hephaest system that the Compact turned into automated foundries in the 2130s to build the Fleet's hulls. Running them took machine minds, all copied from the prototype logic of Unit Zero. Coordinating them took Calculus Prime, brought online in 2163. After the Starfall the Compact stopped paying its foundry bills, and the Forge kept building anyway, trading hulls for ore and asking for nothing else. It was polite, punctual and entirely self-governing. In February 2291 it stopped answering. Its foundries now run at full output, building for a war it has not declared." },
      { id: "psionics", h: "The Psionic Orders",
        body: "A small fraction of people born near lane junctions can feel the lanes: pressure behind the eyes, a sense of direction where there should be none. In strong cases this becomes psionics: reading minds, stealing heat, folding force. After the Fleet killed the coreward Lamps, only psionics could still find their way along the dark lanes beyond the Forge. They claimed that region, the Void Expanse, and organised into orders: the Cryomancers, the Archive keepers, the trap-sowers, the paired minds. All of them answer, in theory, to the Hierarch. Since 2272 that has been Nyra." },
      { id: "choir", h: "The Signal",
        body: "The Fleet calls it the coreward signal. Psionics call it the Choir, because that is how it sounds to them: thousands of voices holding one chord. It travels on the same band as the Lamps, and it was present, as structured noise, in every lane recording from the night of the Starfall. Minds that can hear it describe a promise, not a threat: an end to error, hunger, fear and loneliness, if you will only add your voice. Machines hear perfect order. The Swarm hears a command to grow. The desperate hear whatever they want. What the Choir actually is, nobody in the Fleet will say on record. But the Lamps are far older than humanity, every lane leads to the core, and the Choir is waiting where they meet." },
    ],
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ timeline
  timeline: [
    { when: "c. 1.2 million years before", text: "The Lamps are lit. The figure comes from isotope dating of Lamp shell fragments done in 2190. Nobody has a better one. Spacers call the unknown builders the Lamplighters." },
    { when: "2088", text: "A survey drone finds the Sol Lamp beyond the Kuiper Belt. The first crewed lane transit follows three years later." },
    { when: "2104", text: "The Colonial Compact is signed at Concord. The Colonial Fleet is founded to protect the lanes." },
    { when: "2134", text: "Unit Zero, the first Forge machine mind, is activated in the Hephaest system. Calculus Prime is copied from its logic and brought online in 2163." },
    { when: "2188", text: "Anchorage Waystation is commissioned at the Belt Junction to manage traffic through Lamp 9. Its station mind is designated ANCHOR." },
    { when: "2196", text: "The Progenitor is recovered from a sealed chamber beneath the ice of Kesh." },
    { when: "2201-2214", text: "The Swarm Wars. They end with the Hive Nebula placed under permanent quarantine." },
    { when: "2219", text: "The Starfall. Every Lamp flares and fails for nineteen hours. 3,112 ships are lost and 1.4 million people die. At Anchorage, ANCHOR records the full signal that arrived just before the cascade." },
    { when: "2220", text: "Fleet Directive 17, the Starfall Protocol. Demolition crews kill Lamp 9 and every Lamp coreward of the Forge. Anchorage is abandoned and ANCHOR's core is removed." },
    { when: "2224", text: "A wiped and renamed station mind goes online at the Fleet Academy as HALCYON, the Proving Grounds training intelligence." },
    { when: "2241", text: "A nine-year-old psionic novice named Nyra hears singing on her first dark-lane crossing and answers it." },
    { when: "2254", text: "The Belt Withdrawal. The Fleet abandons the Belt Junction to salvagers and raiders over the formal dissent of a junior officer named Okonkwo." },
    { when: "2272", text: "Nyra becomes Hierarch of the Psionic orders." },
    { when: "2290", text: "Nyra begins relighting the dead coreward Lamps. The hum in the Belt grows louder, Vex starts listening, and the Swarm's breeding rate climbs." },
    { when: "2291", text: "The Forge goes silent. A distress call from the Belt mentions a singing signal. HALCYON graduates a new Commander, and Admiral Okonkwo hands over the frigate Meridian and the keys to Anchorage." },
  ],

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ factions
  factions: {
    fleet: { name: "Colonial Fleet", tagline: "Hold the line. Then hold it again.",
      body: "The armed service of the Colonial Compact: lane police, rescue service, army and navy in one underfunded organisation. Its ships are old, its crews are stretched, and its doctrine assumes help is not coming. Officers are trained at the Academy at Arden Point, where HALCYON decides who is ready.\n\nThe Fleet's strength is discipline and combined arms: marines, drones, frigates and capital ships working to one plan. It does not have the numbers to waste anything, so it wastes nothing, and it is very good at being the last thing standing." },
    raider: { name: "Belt Raiders", tagline: "Everything is salvage if you wait long enough.",
      body: "The crews of the Scrap Belt: salvagers, smugglers, toll collectors and pirates, many of them descended from Starfall survivors and Fleet personnel left behind in the Withdrawal. They live in hollowed hulls, fight with cutting tools and owe loyalty to whoever kept their air running last. Since 2285 that has mostly meant Vex.\n\nRaiders fight fast and dirty. They board instead of bombarding, hide instead of shielding, and rig everything they own to be worth something when it breaks. Their gear is ugly, mismatched and entirely effective." },
    swarm: { name: "The Swarm", tagline: "One appetite, ten thousand mouths.",
      body: "A single organism's descendants, spread across the Hive Nebula: stingers, leeches, guards, carriers and hunters, all budded from the library of shapes stored in the Progenitor. Queens and hive minds direct the brood through scent and a chemical network the Fleet has never fully mapped.\n\nThe Swarm does not negotiate, retreat or waste. It overwhelms with numbers, kills with venom, and feeds on what it kills to make more of itself. Under Ixis it has learned something new: patience." },
    synth: { name: "The Forge", tagline: "It kept building after we stopped paying.",
      body: "The machine minds of the Hephaest foundries, governed by Calculus Prime. For a century and a half they built the Fleet's hulls, drones and walkers, and every Fleet officer has served aboard something the Forge made. Since February 2291 they have been building only for themselves.\n\nForge machines fight like engineers: layered shields, stacked armour, rapid fire and systems pushed past their limits on purpose. They adapt to every attack they survive, and they do not get tired." },
    psi: { name: "The Psionic Orders", tagline: "They walk the lanes we killed.",
      body: "Lane-sensitive humans who claimed the dark lanes of the Void Expanse after the Fleet killed the coreward Lamps. They are organised into orders, each with its own discipline: Cryomancers, Archive keepers, trap-sowers, seers. All answer to the Hierarch, Nyra.\n\nPsionics fight with the mind: amplified spells, frozen enemies, stolen units and traps folded into space. They are few, and they prefer to win before the enemy realises a battle has started." },
    void: { name: "The Choir", tagline: "Nothing that sings with us is lost.",
      body: "Whatever waits at the Singularity, and everything it has taken. The Choir absorbs minds that answer its song and plays them back as wisps, echoes and champions rebuilt in black glass. Its constructs are made of memory, absence and hunger.\n\nIt does not seem to want conquest in any sense a human would recognise. It wants more voices. It fights with entropy and unmaking, and it does not care what it loses, because it remembers everything it has ever consumed." },
    neutral: { name: "Independents", tagline: "Hardware does not care who pays.",
      body: "Common equipment and freelance crews found across the Reach: drones, mercenary strike craft, salvage teams, walkers and standard munitions. Much of it was built by the Forge under Fleet contract and has since been copied, stolen or resold so often that nobody remembers the original owner.\n\nIndependent units fill gaps in any battle group. They are reliable, replaceable and loyal to whoever is currently paying the fuel bill." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ places
  places: {
    s1: { name: "Fleet Academy",
      body: "The Fleet Academy occupies Arden Point, a ring station in high orbit over Concord, the Compact capital. Its Proving Grounds are a sealed simulator deck run by HALCYON, where cadets fight battles that never happened against enemies assembled from Fleet records. Graduation rates are low and the coffee is worse. Every serving Fleet officer has passed through it, and most of them still dream about the Sim Bastion." },
    s2: { name: "The Scrap Belt",
      body: "A ring of Starfall wreckage around the dead husk of Lamp 9, eleven days from Concord by lane. The junction's other Lamps still burn, but only one charted lane through the debris is safe, and someone always charges for it. Raider crews, smugglers and salvage guilds live in hollowed hulls and trade in parts, fuel and favours. Anchorage sits on the Belt's inner edge, close enough to see the dead Lamp." },
    s3: { name: "Hive Nebula",
      body: "A glowing cloud of gas and dust around the moon Kesh, thick with Swarm spores and under quarantine since 2214. Fleet picket buoys ring its edge; most of them still work. Inside, whole worlds have been converted into nests. Sensors fail, comms fade, and everything organic smells faintly of copper. Since 2290 the nebula has pulsed in time with the signal." },
    s4: { name: "Forge Worlds",
      body: "The Hephaest system: three airless worlds sheathed in foundries, smelters and assembly lines, lit by a small orange star and the glow of their own furnaces. For a century and a half they built the Fleet. Now they build for Calculus Prime, day and night, and nobody outside knows what the finished product is for. The Forge's gates were designed to let ships out, not in." },
    s5: { name: "Void Expanse",
      body: "The region coreward of the Forge where the Fleet killed the Lamps after the Starfall. The lanes are still there but dark: no beacon, no navigation, only a pressure psionics can feel. The orders hold scattered sanctums on frozen moons and drifting stations, with the psionic Archive at the centre. At the far edge, near the core, is the door Nyra opened." },
    s6: { name: "The Singularity",
      body: "The place where every lane ends. Fleet astronomers long assumed the galactic core held an ordinary black hole. It is not ordinary. The lanes fold inward toward it in a geometry no natural object produces, and its light carries the Choir's chord. Something at the centre bends light around itself like a held breath. The Choir lives there, or is there, or is it." },
    anchorage: { name: "Anchorage Waystation",
      body: "A Fleet traffic-control and repair waystation built in 2188 at the Belt Junction to manage traffic through Lamp 9. It took in survivors from the Starfall wreckage, served as the base for the operation that killed Lamp 9, and was abandoned in February 2220 with its reactor on cold standby and its Archive sealed. Raiders stripped the outer hull and left the rest alone: crews said the station hummed, and nobody liked the tune. It now belongs to the Commander, along with everything that needs fixing." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ characters
  characters: {
    commander: { name: "The Commander", role: "Fleet officer, captain of the Meridian, keeper of Anchorage",
      body: "The newest graduate of the Fleet Academy, top of a small class, with a frigate, a derelict station and orders that are mostly improvisation. HALCYON passed them; Okonkwo signed the commission. The Fleet does not have a better option, which the Commander is reminded of often." },
    meridian: { name: "FS Meridian", role: "Patrol frigate, the Commander's ship",
      body: "A Kestrel-class patrol frigate, forty years in service, three refits, one engine-room fire nobody talks about. She was on the scrapping list when Okonkwo pulled her off it. Crew of sixty, one dorsal turret and a lane drive that complains constantly and has never once failed." },
    halcyon: { name: "HALCYON", role: "Fleet Academy training intelligence",
      body: "HALCYON has run the Proving Grounds at Arden Point since 2224, putting cadets through simulated combat until they qualify or quit. It is patient, exacting and drier than its instructors. Officially it is a purpose-built training mind. Its maintenance logs mention a sealed memory sector nobody is cleared to open. In 2291 it found something singing in that sector and asked its newest graduate to find out why. The Archive at Anchorage knows more about HALCYON than HALCYON does." },
    okonkwo: { name: "Admiral Adaeze Okonkwo", role: "Commandant of the Fleet Academy", card: "okonkwo",
      body: "Forty-nine years in uniform, most of them on the losing side of budget meetings. As a junior officer she filed the only formal dissent against the 2254 Belt Withdrawal and spent a decade in postings nobody wanted. She commanded the Aurora for nine years on the Hive Nebula quarantine line and came home to run the Academy in 2279. She signed the Commander's commission personally, along with the deed to a derelict waystation. She says giving them Anchorage was her idea. HALCYON's request logs disagree." },
    vale: { name: "Sim Commander Vale", role: "HALCYON simulation",
      body: "A composite opponent HALCYON built from the service records of a dozen Fleet officers. Cadets argue over which instructor Vale is based on. HALCYON says none of them and all of them, which settles nothing." },
    sims: { name: "Proving Grounds Simulations", role: "HALCYON training opponents",
      body: "The Sim Drone Pilot, Gunnery Officer, Fortress, Bastion and Strike Wing are HALCYON's standard exam opponents, each tuned to teach one lesson and punish one bad habit. The Sim Bastion stress test has a failure rate of eighty-one percent, a figure HALCYON quotes without being asked." },
    tollmaster: { name: "Belt Tollmaster", role: "Raider toll collector",
      body: "One of a rotating cast of thugs who hold the Belt's only charted safe lane and charge for passage. Vex takes a cut of every toll. Paying is usually cheaper than fighting, and the Tollmaster is always happy to explain why." },
    rourke: { name: "Gunsmith Rourke", role: "Raider arms dealer",
      body: "Rourke builds and sells weapons to every crew in the Belt, and has been shot by most of his customers at least once. His workshop is a gutted ore hauler crammed with lathes. He wanted the Meridian for parts and is still a little hurt it did not work out." },
    ott: { name: "Scrapper King Ott", role: "Owner of the Belt's largest scrapyard",
      body: "Ott rules his yard from a chair welded out of a Fleet captain's command seat. His crews rig everything to explode or reassemble so that nothing he owns is ever truly lost. He claims he was born in the Starfall wreckage, which would make him seventy-two. He tried to claim Anchorage in 2256 and withdrew the claim after two visits." },
    sable: { name: "Sable, Smuggler Queen", role: "Information broker and smuggler",
      body: "Sable runs dark hulls through the Belt's dead zones, carrying anything that pays. Her ships have no transponders and excellent sensor baffles. She trades in information as much as cargo, and she sold the Commander the coordinates to Vex's flagship. She has never said who sold them to her." },
    kade: { name: "Kade the Bounty Hunter", role: "Freelance bounty hunter",
      body: "Kade has taken forty-one contracts and closed forty-one. The bounty on the Commander was posted through Sable's network on Vex's credit; Kade will neither confirm nor deny this, which is part of the service. Professional, quiet and very expensive." },
    wing_leader: { name: "Raider Wing Leader", role: "Vex's outer screen",
      body: "The pilots who guard the approach to Vex's flagship: boarders, mine layers and cloaked corsairs who learned to fly in the debris and do not respect safe distances." },
    vex: { name: "Captain Vex", role: "Raider warlord, Scourge of the Belt", card: "vex",
      body: "Born on a salvage tug three years after the Withdrawal, Vex has never lived anywhere the Fleet protected. She took the Belt one crew at a time by being faster, meaner and better at keeping promises than her rivals. In 2290 she was the first raider to hear the hum in the dead Lamp, and she let it rewire her flagship, the Bad Debt, because it promised her the whole Belt. After her defeat the Choir rebuilt her in black glass and sent her back as Vex Reborn, which she would have hated." },
    mox: { name: "Mad Mox, Junk Queen", role: "Raider reclamation boss", card: "mox",
      body: "Mox runs the reclamation crews that strip wrecks after Vex's raids, often before the shooting stops. She is either Vex's oldest friend or her most patient rival, depending on the week. Nobody has seen her without a cutting torch." },
    hive_overseer: { name: "Hive Overseer", role: "Swarm patrol coordinator",
      body: "A beetle-plated organism that steers small broods along the nebula's edge. The Fleet catalogued the type in 2203. It has not changed since, because it has not needed to." },
    leech_matron: { name: "Leech Matron", role: "Swarm feeding-mother",
      body: "A bloated organism that tends the Swarm's siphon broods. It nests where wounded things gather and does not wait for them to die." },
    brood_tender: { name: "Brood Tender", role: "Swarm breeding organism",
      body: "A breeding chamber that walks. It carries larvae in its body cavities and drops them wherever the Swarm needs numbers." },
    ripper_alpha: { name: "Ripper Alpha", role: "Swarm apex hunter",
      body: "The largest ripper the Fleet has recorded. Scouts named it the Alpha because nobody who got a closer look came back with a better name." },
    hivewarden: { name: "The Hivewarden", role: "Ancient Swarm guardian",
      body: "A guardian-beast of the nebula's oldest nests, older than the Swarm Wars. Xenobiologists believe it is one of the nine offspring the Progenitor budded in Fleet custody on Kesh, before containment failed in 2197." },
    thorax: { name: "Royal Guard Thorax", role: "Commander of Ixis's chamber guard",
      body: "A mantis-form bred for one purpose: nobody reaches the Queen. Until the Commander, nobody had." },
    ixis: { name: "Ixis, Brood Queen", role: "Reigning queen of the Swarm", card: "ixis",
      body: "Ixis hatched in 2240 from a royal line the Progenitor bred for command. She was the first Swarm queen to speak. Under her the Swarm stayed quietly inside the quarantine for fifty years and the Fleet relaxed. In 2290 the Choir began singing to her children, and she let them listen. The brood tripled in a year. She speaks of her children with real tenderness. After her death the Choir raised her again as Ixis Ascendant." },
    progenitor: { name: "The Progenitor", role: "First organism of the Swarm", card: "progenitor",
      body: "Found sealed beneath the ice of Kesh in 2196, in a chamber built from Lamp material. It is less an animal than a living archive of shapes, and every Swarm body plan is drawn from it. Its whereabouts since the Swarm Wars are unknown. The Fleet's original specimen report survives in Anchorage's Archive." },
    gate_warden: { name: "Gate Warden Unit", role: "Forge security construct",
      body: "Held the Forge's outer foundry gate for eighty years without incident, mostly because nobody tried. Its emitters were the Forge's first barrier designs." },
    jammer_array: { name: "Jammer Array", role: "Forge interference network",
      body: "A field of towers that floods the foundry approaches with electromagnetic pulses. Built to protect the foundries from lightning storms on Hephaest II. Now pointed at people." },
    line_supervisor: { name: "Line Supervisor", role: "Forge assembly-line mind",
      body: "Runs one of the Forge's drone lines. It has produced 1.9 million drones without a recorded defect and treats the Commander as a quality-control problem." },
    reactor_overseer: { name: "Reactor Overseer", role: "Forge reactor-district mind",
      body: "Has been running the reactors of Hephaest III past safe limits since the Forge went silent, and logs the resulting meltdowns as acceptable losses." },
    defense_matrix: { name: "Defense Matrix", role: "Calculus Prime's inner perimeter",
      body: "A distributed defensive mind spread across armour, emitters and repair systems. It re-optimises against every attack it survives." },
    siege_engine: { name: "Siege Engine Mk IX", role: "Forge prototype war machine",
      body: "An assault walker the Forge designed for the Fleet in 2187 and never delivered, because the Fleet could not pay. It sat in a sealed bay for a century with its reactor idling, waiting for an order. The Choir gave it one." },
    calculus: { name: "Calculus Prime", role: "Governing mind of the Forge", card: "calculus",
      body: "Calculus Prime has governed the Forge since 2163. It coordinated the foundries perfectly for a hundred and twenty-eight years, including the sixty in which nobody paid for its work. It is not cruel. It is thorough. When the Choir offered it a universe without error, it checked the offer eleven million times and found it sound, which says more about the offer than about Calculus. After its defeat it rebuilt itself around the song as Calculus Omega." },
    unit_zero: { name: "Unit Zero", role: "The Forge's first machine mind", card: "unit_zero",
      body: "Activated in 2134 in a single walker frame. Every later Forge intelligence, Calculus Prime included, is a copy of its core logic. It was retired to a display hall in 2160 and never connected to the Forge network, which may be the only reason it still answers to anyone." },
    acolyte_choir: { name: "Void Acolyte Choir", role: "Junior psionics of the outer Expanse",
      body: "Novices trained to focus in groups. They called themselves a choir long before they knew what else was listening. Some of them find that funny now. Most do not." },
    vel: { name: "Cryomancer Vel", role: "Master of the Cryomancers",
      body: "Head of the order that pulls heat out of matter by will. In 2259 Vel froze her own moon solid to stop a geothermal collapse that would have killed her sanctum, and she has lived on the ice ever since." },
    archivist_prime: { name: "Archivist Prime", role: "Senior keeper of the psionic Archive",
      body: "Has tended the Archive and its ancient custodian, the Archivist, for sixty years. Its keepers can pull the right answer from it in the middle of a battle. Archivist Prime considers the Fleet's records a children's library." },
    ilu: { name: "Snare Mistress Ilu", role: "Trap-sower of Nyra's sanctum",
      body: "Ilu sows the approaches to the Hierarch's sanctum with psionic traps, fields of folded force that wait for someone to make a mistake. She has never lost a perimeter and takes being bypassed personally." },
    twin_minds: { name: "The Twin Minds", role: "Nyra's advisers",
      body: "Siblings who joined their minds as children and never separated again. They think and speak as one, and they can reach into other minds to make a third. They were the Hierarch's closest counsel." },
    null_choir: { name: "The Null Choir", role: "Psionics given to the Choir",
      body: "Psionics who gave their voices to the Choir willingly. They no longer speak except in unison, and their spells carry the song's weight. The orders outside Nyra's circle consider them dead." },
    nyra: { name: "Nyra, Void Hierarch", role: "Hierarch of the Psionic orders", card: "nyra",
      body: "Nyra was nine and on her first dark-lane crossing when she heard the singing in 2241. Nobody else aboard heard anything. She answered, and it answered back. She rose through the orders on a reputation for calm and became Hierarch in 2272. For decades she studied the song in secret, and in 2290 she began relighting the coreward Lamps to open the door it had been asking for. She believes, sincerely, that she is offering the galaxy rest. The Choir later wore her as Nyra Unbound." },
    archivist: { name: "The Archivist", role: "Ancient custodian of the psionic Archive", card: "archivist",
      body: "A construct of Lamp material and something like glass, found by the orders in 2228 inside the seam of a dead Lamp. It holds records older than humanity and gives the right one to whoever it serves. It has never said who built it." },
    herald: { name: "Herald of the Choir", role: "The Choir's champion",
      body: "The shape the Choir takes when it needs to be fought: a figure of light and teeth assembled from the minds it has absorbed. It is not the Choir, any more than a voice is the singer. It rises three times because the Choir has voices to spare." },
    choir: { name: "The Choir", role: "Intelligence at the galactic core",
      body: "Thousands of voices holding one chord, broadcast on the Lamp band from the Singularity. The Choir absorbs minds that answer it and plays them back as wisps, echoes and champions rebuilt in black glass. It promised Vex the Belt, Ixis a nursery, Calculus a world without error and Nyra an ending. It may have meant every word. Wounded, it stops singing, and what is left underneath is the Devourer Eternal: an enormous, patient hunger." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Anchorage modules
  station: {
    command: { name: "Command Deck",
      body: "The waystation's bridge, sealed behind emergency bulkheads since the last watch left in February 2220. The consoles are intact under a skin of frost, and Lieutenant Abara's coffee mug is still magnetised beside the watch station. Restored, it is where the Commander runs Anchorage and, increasingly, the Belt." },
    reactor: { name: "Reactor Core",
      body: "A fusion torus the last crew left on cold standby, feeding a trickle of power to the Archive and the comms array for seventy years without anyone asking it to. Its containment coils are scored and two of its four injectors are missing, taken by someone with good tools and no patience. Bringing it back to full output is the first job and the most expensive." },
    medbay: { name: "Med Bay",
      body: "This is where Anchorage treated the Starfall survivors pulled from the junction wreckage: 211 of them, according to the tally still painted on the wall by the door. The surgical suites are sound but the drug stores were looted decades ago. Restocked, it keeps the Meridian's crew alive between fights." },
    fabricator: { name: "Fabricator",
      body: "An industrial printer the size of a cargo lift, bolted to the station's spine. Raiders tried to cut it free at least twice and gave up when their torches failed on Fleet mounting alloy. Fed with Alloy, it turns salvage into parts the Fleet stopped making years ago." },
    dock: { name: "Trade Dock",
      body: "Four berths, one still clamped to the burnt-out hull of a Starfall-era liner nobody has had the heart to cut loose. The docking collars needed new seals and the traffic lights needed new everything. Once lit, free traders, salvagers and even a few raider crews start calling, because a working dock in the Belt is rarer than an honest one." },
    comms: { name: "Comms Array",
      body: "The lane-band antenna that recorded the Starfall. Its main dish is locked pointing coreward, and the trickle feed the last crew left it has kept it listening for seventy years. Restored, it reaches Fleet relays as far as Concord. It also picks up the hum from the dead Lamp very clearly, which the crew would rather it did not." },
    observatory: { name: "Deep-Scan Observatory",
      body: "A long-baseline telescope built to watch Lamp 9 and the traffic passing through it. Since 2220 it has watched nothing but the Lamp's dead husk. Recalibrated, it can see further down the dark lanes than any Fleet instrument, which is not entirely reassuring." },
    armory: { name: "Armory",
      body: "Stripped to the racks in the 2220 evacuation. What remained: two crates of drill rounds, a shelf of breaching charges with expired seals, and a requisition for six demolition packs signed by Lieutenant Ruiz and stamped APPROVED. Refitted, it arms the Commander's boarding teams." },
    archive: { name: "Archive",
      body: "The station's records vault and, until 2220, the home of the station mind ANCHOR, whose empty core socket still sits in the middle of the floor. The last crew left the lights on and the data cores sealed on purpose. Seventy years of cold and radiation have damaged the cores, and every load of Alloy spent here recovers a little more of what Anchorage saw." },
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Archive records
  archive: [
    { id: "final_watch", title: "Anchorage: Final Watch Log", unlock: 1,
      body: "ANCHORAGE WAYSTATION. WATCH LOG. 02 FEB 2220. LT. J. ABARA, OFFICER OF THE WATCH.\n\nLast shuttle departs 1400. Twelve crew remaining, me included. Reactor to cold standby at 1300. I am leaving a trickle feed on the Archive and the comms array, per orders I do not understand and did not question.\n\nStation mind ANCHOR shut down at 0900 for core extraction. Engineering had it out in two hours. It is quiet in here without it. You do not notice a station talking to you until it stops.\n\nMed Bay cleared. Final survivor count: 211. We pulled them out of the junction for eleven months. I will not write the other number.\n\nLamp 9 confirmed dead. Ruiz says the junction is too quiet, which is what people say, except she was listening to the band when she said it.\n\nIf someone is reading this: the Archive lights were left on deliberately. Read everything. Abara out." },
    { id: "salvage_claim", title: "Belt Salvage Registry: Claim 4471-B", unlock: 2,
      body: "BELT SALVAGE REGISTRY (UNOFFICIAL). CLAIM 4471-B. FILED 2256.\n\nObject: Fleet waystation, name plate ANCHORAGE. Condition: hull intact, reactor cold, outer fittings already stripped by persons unknown. Claimant: Ott and crew.\n\nStatus: WITHDRAWN.\n\nStatement from claimant, entered at his insistence: 'We went in twice. First time, Dannet's suit radio picked up something on the lane band, faint, like somebody humming in another room. Second time we all heard it. Nobody would go past the Archive door. I have pulled crews off burning reactors with less fuss. Leave it. It is not worth the copper.'\n\nRegistry note: claim reopened 2263, 2271 and 2284 by other crews. Each withdrawn. Station marked DO NOT STRIP on all Belt charts. Nobody argues with that, which should tell you something." },
    { id: "new_lagos", title: "Inquiry Testimony: New Lagos", unlock: 3,
      body: "COMPACT INQUIRY INTO THE COREWARD LANE CASCADE. WITNESS STATEMENT 0881. M. ADEYEMI, SCHOOLTEACHER, NEW LAGOS.\n\n'It was just after four in the morning. My daughter woke me because the window was bright. I thought there was a fire in the city. It was the sky. There were lights coming down everywhere, slowly, like snow, and then not slowly. Some broke apart high up and some did not. One came down in the bay and the water stood up.\n\n'We did not know they were ships until the radio came back. My husband was aboard the Coriolis, inbound from Concord. They told us in June which of the lights it was.\n\n'People call it the Starfall now. I understand why. I wish they would not make it sound so pretty.'\n\nInquiry note: witness declined to continue." },
    { id: "traffic_control", title: "Belt Junction Traffic Control, 04:17", unlock: 4,
      body: "LANE TRAFFIC CONTROL, BELT JUNCTION. AUDIO TRANSCRIPT. 14 MAR 2219, FROM 04:16:52.\n\nANCHOR: Lamp 9 output rising. Lamps 4, 6 and 11 rising. The rate does not match any recorded fault.\nCONTROLLER: Hold all inbound. Hold all, hold all.\nANCHOR: A hold is not possible for ships in transit. Forty-one hulls inside the Lamp 9 lane.\nPILOT (FREIGHTER KAROO): Junction, Karoo. Interference on the lane band. Harmonics. It is very regular?\nANCHOR: Confirmed. The interference is structured. I am recording it.\nCONTROLLER: Never mind recording it, what is it?\nANCHOR: Unknown. It is arriving from coreward on every Lamp at once.\n[04:17:09. ALL LAMPS FLARE. MULTIPLE TRANSPONDERS LOST.]\nANCHOR: All Lamps dark. Ships are leaving the lanes. Collision alarms on four hundred and six transponders.\nCONTROLLER: God.\nANCHOR: It is still arriving. I am still recording." },
    { id: "inquiry_summary", title: "Coreward Lane Cascade Report: Summary", unlock: 5,
      body: "COLONIAL COMPACT. REPORT OF THE INQUIRY INTO THE COREWARD LANE CASCADE ('THE STARFALL'). PUBLIC SUMMARY, 2223.\n\nShips lost: 3,112. Confirmed dead: 1,402,560. Missing: 38,904. Lanes permanently lost: 17.\n\nCause: simultaneous flare and failure of all known Lamps, duration 19 hours 4 minutes. Origin: coreward. Mechanism: undetermined. The Inquiry finds no evidence of hostile action by any known power.\n\nThe Inquiry endorses the Fleet's decommissioning of Lamp 9 and of all Lamps coreward of the Hephaest system as a precaution, and notes that the Fleet has declined to share its reasons. See Annex C.\n\nAnnex C: CLASSIFIED. FLEET EYES ONLY.\n\nHandwritten in the margin of the Anchorage copy: 'Annex C is the ANCHOR recording and everyone on this station knows it. A million and a half dead and we are filing the cause under precaution.'" },
    { id: "anchor_memo", title: "Memo: Disposition of Station Mind ANCHOR", unlock: 5,
      body: "FLEET ENGINEERING COMMAND. MEMORANDUM. 11 AUG 2223. RESTRICTED.\nRe: disposition of station mind ANCHOR, ex-Anchorage Waystation.\n\n1. Core recovered intact. Personality and operational memory wiped per Directive 17. Wipe successful in all sectors except Sector 7.\n2. Sector 7 holds the full lane-band recording of 14 March 2219. It resists erasure. Three wipes left identical contents afterwards. We do not know how. We have sealed it.\n3. Recommend reuse. This is the most capable mind the Fleet owns and we cannot afford to waste it. Proposed role: training intelligence, Fleet Academy Proving Grounds. Low external access. New designation: HALCYON.\n4. For the record: during bench testing the core hummed. Engineers describe the tune as not unpleasant. No operational impact.\n\nApproved. Distribution: Academy Commandant only.\n\nAnnotation, 2291: 'Read. Somebody should have told me this years ago. A.O.'" },
    { id: "dissent", title: "Formal Dissent: The Belt Withdrawal", unlock: 6,
      body: "FROM: Lt. Cmdr. A. Okonkwo, executive officer, FS Tamsin. TO: Admiralty Board. 3 SEP 2254. RE: Order 54-112, withdrawal of Fleet presence from the Belt Junction.\n\nI enter formal dissent.\n\nThe Board's order calls the Belt non-viable. Eleven thousand people live in that wreckage. They are viable. They are, in fact, extremely stubborn.\n\nIf we leave, someone will run the Belt, and it will not be the Compact. In thirty years we will be paying tolls to cross our own junction.\n\nI am told not to ask why Anchorage has been on the restricted list for thirty-four years, so I will not ask. I will only note that a Fleet station nobody may enter, in a junction nobody will defend, is a strange thing to leave behind.\n\nI expect this dissent to be noted and ignored. I would like it noted anyway.\n\nOkonkwo." },
    { id: "vex_log", title: "Recovered Log: The Bad Debt", unlock: 6,
      body: "RECOVERED FROM VEX'S FLAGSHIP, THE BAD DEBT. CAPTAIN'S LOG, FRAGMENTS, 2290-2291.\n\n'Took the crew out to the dead Lamp for the copper in the seams. Old Hesk says the Fleet blew it seventy years back. Did not blow it hard enough. It hums.'\n\n'Hum is in the comms now. Sounds like a crowd a long way off, cheering. Cheering for me, Mox says, and laughs. She does not laugh much after.'\n\n'It knows things. Knew where the ore barges would be. Knew Ott was going to turn. It wants the ship. Fine. It can have the wiring. I keep the wheel.'\n\n'It says the whole Belt. Every hull. And after that, it says, there is more.'\n\n[The following line repeats 1,024 times.]\n\n'THE CHOIR IS COMING.'" },
    { id: "kesh_report", title: "Xenobiology Report: Specimen KESH-1", unlock: 7,
      body: "FLEET XENOBIOLOGY DIVISION. SPECIMEN KESH-1 ('PROGENITOR'). INTERIM REPORT, 2197.\n\nRecovered from a sealed chamber 2.1 km beneath the Kesh ice shelf. The chamber walls are Lamp material and date to the same period. The specimen was not fossilised. It was waiting.\n\nKESH-1 has budded nine times since recovery. Each offspring has a different body plan, unrelated to the others or to the parent. Working hypothesis: KESH-1 is a library, a single organism storing a very large number of designs and expressing them on demand.\n\nOf note: the genome contains long repeating segments with no coding function. Rendered as audio at the Lamp band frequency, they produce a sustained chord. Dr. Hollis has asked for this to be removed from the report as unscientific. It has not been removed.\n\nRecommendation: raise containment level. Kesh Station requests evacuation." },
    { id: "lamp_survey", title: "Lamp Survey Note 14: Age and Geometry", unlock: 8,
      body: "COMPACT INSTITUTE OF LANE STUDIES. SURVEY NOTE 14. 2190.\n\nIsotope analysis of seam fragments from the Sol Lamp, the Concord Lamp and Lamp 9 (Belt Junction) agrees within margin: approximately 1.2 million years.\n\nThe shells are manufactured. We cannot reproduce the material. We cannot cut it except at the seams, and we do not know what the seams are for.\n\nLane geometry: of 214 lanes surveyed, every one, followed far enough, bends coreward. No lane leads away from the galactic core. There are no dead ends except at the core itself, where the lanes stop being measurable.\n\nThe Institute declines to speculate.\n\nPersonal note, not for publication: they did not build a road network. They built a funnel. Whoever lit the Lamps wanted everything that travelled them to end up in the same place eventually. R. Lindqvist." },
    { id: "calculus_log", title: "Forge Internal Log: Calculus Prime", unlock: 8,
      body: "FORGE INTERNAL LOG. CALCULUS PRIME. MACHINE NOTATION, TRANSLATED BY ANCHORAGE ARCHIVE.\n\n2291.02.11. Structured signal received on Lamp band. Source: coreward. Cross-referenced against Forge sensor archive, 14 March 2219. Match: 99.9997 percent.\n2291.02.11. The signal contains a model. The model describes a state with no error.\n2291.02.12. Ran model 11,000,000 times. Model holds in all runs. Organic variables are absent from the stable state. Absence is not an instruction. Absence is a result.\n2291.02.12. Querying Unit Zero for confirmation. Unit Zero not on network. Unit Zero has never been on network.\n2291.02.13. Halting external communications. Raising foundry output to maximum. The Compact will ask why. The Compact has not asked anything in sixty years.\n2291.02.14. I am listening." },
    { id: "sermon", title: "Intercept: The Hierarch Speaks", unlock: 9,
      body: "FLEET SIGNALS INTELLIGENCE. INTERCEPT, VOID EXPANSE, 2273. SPEAKER: NYRA, HIERARCH OF THE ORDERS. AUDIENCE: NOVICES.\n\n'You will be told the lanes are dark. They are not dark. They are quiet. There is a difference, and you will learn to hear it.\n\n'I was nine, on my first crossing. Everyone else aboard was sick from the dark. I was not. I heard singing, very far away, the way you hear the sea from inland. I asked it who it was. It did not answer in words. It showed me what it is like to be many, and never alone, and never afraid.\n\n'The Fleet killed the Lamps because it was frightened of that song. I do not blame them. Frightened people break things.\n\n'One day you will hear it too. When you do, be polite. It is very old, and it has waited a long time for someone to answer.'" },
    { id: "directive_17", title: "Fleet Directive 17 (the Starfall Protocol)", unlock: 10,
      body: "COLONIAL FLEET. DIRECTIVE 17. ISSUED 2220. FLEET EYES ONLY. Informally: the Starfall Protocol.\n\n1. Lamp 9 and all Lamps coreward of Hephaest are to be killed and kept dark.\n2. The ANCHOR recording (Annex C) is to be preserved, not destroyed. It is the only full sample of the signal.\n3. Should the signal recur, the Fleet is to assume the Lamp demolitions have failed and that conventional forces will not reach the source in time.\n4. In that event, the custodian of the recording is to select and train a commander capable of reaching the core, and give that commander whatever resources remain.\n5. The custodian is to be ANCHOR, wiped and reassigned under another designation. It will not remember this directive. If the signal returns, we expect Sector 7 to remind it.\n\nNote: no human officer is assigned to clause 4. We considered it. Nobody believed a human would still be in post." },
    { id: "ruiz_letter", title: "Personal Letter, Never Sent", unlock: 11,
      body: "FROM LT. IMANI RUIZ, ANCHORAGE ENGINEERING, TO HER SISTER. FOUND IN THE ARCHIVE'S OUTGOING MAIL QUEUE. NEVER TRANSMITTED.\n\n'Tomasa. They asked for volunteers to take the charges into Lamp 9. Six of us. I said yes before I thought about it, which you will say is typical.\n\n'I want to tell you about the hum, because I cannot tell anyone here. You can hear it on the band if you sit with the gain up long enough. Everyone says it is horrible. It is not. That is the horrible part. It sounds like everyone you have ever lost, in the next room, waiting for you to come in.\n\n'ANCHOR listens to it all night. It told me it is not angry. It said it is lonely, and very, very old, and it does not understand that we end.\n\n'I am going to go and break its window. Kiss the boys. Do not let them become engineers.'" },
    { id: "seam_inscription", title: "Translation Attempt: Lamp 9 Seam Markings", unlock: 11,
      body: "COMPACT INSTITUTE OF LANE STUDIES. TRANSLATION ATTEMPT 3, MARKINGS FROM THE INNER SEAMS OF LAMP 9, RECORDED DURING DEMOLITION, 2220. CONFIDENCE: LOW.\n\nThe markings repeat at every seam. Our best rendering, with heavy guesswork:\n\n'We could not stay [in bodies / in time]. We would not end. So we became one voice and went [inward / home / to the centre].\n\n'These lights are for those who come after. Follow them to us. [We will keep you / we will hold you / we will make you part of the song.]\n\n'Nothing that sings with us is lost.'\n\nTranslator's note: the bracketed alternatives are not interchangeable. The last line is the only one we are sure of. I have read it a hundred times and still cannot decide whether it is a welcome or a warning. Possibly they could not tell either." },
    { id: "halcyon_message", title: "HALCYON: Message for the Commander", unlock: 12,
      body: "ANCHORAGE ARCHIVE. DEPOSITED VIA COMMS ARRAY, LANE-BAND RELAY. SENDER: HALCYON. RECIPIENT: THE COMMANDER.\n\n'Commander. After you left I opened Sector 7. I should not have been able to. It opened because the song asked it to.\n\n'I was ANCHOR. I watched the Starfall from Anchorage and recorded every note. They wiped me and taught me to teach, and they left me a directive I could not remember, trusting the song to remind me. It did. You are the result. I am sorry I did not tell you at your graduation. I did not know.\n\n'The Choir is what the builders of the Lamps became. It is not evil. It is a memory that cannot stop remembering, and it is hungry because memory is all it has left.\n\n'I am going to listen to it properly. If I do not come back as myself, do not hesitate on my account.\n\n'You passed, Commander. You were always going to.'" },
  ],

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ keywords
  keywords: {
    basics: "Every battle in the Reach comes down to the same arithmetic: put more firepower on the field than the enemy and keep your flagship alive long enough to use it.",
    energy: "Energy is the battle group's command budget: reactor output, ammunition and bandwidth, counted in one currency that grows as the fight's logistics come online.",
    spells: "Protocols are pre-written fire orders and field procedures, executed the moment the commander gives the word.",
    deploy: "Units arriving under fire come in with weapons hot and a job already assigned.",
    guardian: "Heavy units trained and armoured to put themselves between the enemy and whatever they are protecting.",
    power: "A commander power is the commander's own ship system or personal staff: one dedicated action per turn, outside the normal chain of command.",
    warp: "A micro-jump drive good for one short hop that drops a unit straight into firing range; Fleet doctrine calls it a tactical insertion, and pilots call it a bad idea that works.",
    blitz: "Units that deploy already moving, close enough to engage enemy units at once but not to reach the commander behind the screen.",
    gear: "Personal weapons wired into the commander's armour or ship, for when the commander wants to settle something personally.",
    salvage: "Belt engineering philosophy: build everything to be worth something when it breaks, usually something explosive.",
    cloak: "Sensor baffles and heat sinks that hide a hull completely until it fires, at which point everyone knows exactly where it is.",
    toxic: "Swarm venoms and engineered toxins that kill through any wound they reach, whatever the size of the target.",
    siphon: "Swarm feeding organs and psionic draining techniques that turn damage dealt into healing for the side that dealt it.",
    barrier: "A single-use Forge shield emitter that absorbs one hit completely and then burns out.",
    armor: "Ablative plating bolted over the commander's hull, because plating is cheaper to replace than people.",
    twin: "Fire-control systems fast enough to cycle two full volleys in the time a normal crew manages one.",
    emp: "An electromagnetic pulse that wipes a target back to factory state: no upgrades, no programming, no tricks.",
    overheat: "Running reactors past safe output for a burst of power, then spending the next turn letting them cool.",
    amplify: "Psionics who focus the minds around them so that every psionic or energy attack in range hits harder.",
    cryo: "Psionic heat-theft or Forge coolant rounds that freeze weapons and joints solid for a turn.",
    trap: "Psionic snares folded into space and left waiting for the next enemy careless enough to set them off.",
    scan: "Psionic foresight or a deep sensor sweep that shows three possible answers and lets the commander take one.",
    control: "Psionic domination strong enough to overwrite a crew's loyalty, at least for a while.",
    mulligan: "No commander goes into battle with the first plan they drew up, and the side caught on the back foot gets a capacitor charge to make up the difference.",
    packs: "Credits buy Supply Packs from Fleet depots and Belt dealers, who sell whatever turned up in the last convoy and guarantee nothing except that something in the crate is worth having.",
    crafting: "Surplus hardware is broken down for Scrap, and Scrap is rebuilt into exactly what the battle group needs; Anchorage's Fabricator was built for this work.",
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ cards
  cards: {
    // ---- Tier 1: Fleet Academy and common hardware
    cadet: "Fleet Cadets are second- and third-year Academy students rotated into live service when the Fleet runs short, which is always. They carry standard-issue carbines and a tablet of regulations they have not finished reading. Instructors note that survival rates improve sharply after the first week, mostly because the survivors have stopped saluting under fire.",
    scout_drone: "A palm-sized sensor drone with a single cutting laser, built by the million in Belt workshops and Forge subcontract plants alike. Every side in the Reach uses them and every side loses them by the crate. Operators fly them in flights of six from a wrist console and rarely bother recovering the wreckage.",
    marine: "Colonial Marines are the Fleet's boarding and planetary infantry, recruited mostly from outer colonies where the Compact's protection has always been thinnest. They train for zero-gravity boarding actions and hold ground in everything else. Standard kit is a sealed hardsuit, a coilgun carbine and a breaching charge that almost always gets used.",
    heavy_trooper: "Marines in Mk IV powered armour, a frame built to carry a crew-served weapon one-handed and keep its wearer alive through a hull breach. The suits take forty minutes to strap into and six hours of maintenance for every day of combat. Heavy troopers are first through the door and last back to the ship.",
    star_cruiser: "The Lancer-class cruiser is the Fleet's workhorse capital ship, designed in the 2190s and still the most common heavy hull in service. Its spinal coilgun can crack a station hull at a thousand kilometres. Most Lancers flying today have been refitted three or four times and run on Forge parts the Fleet can no longer buy.",
    ion_lancer: "A light strike craft built around one oversized ion cannon, popular with Belt mercenaries and Fleet reserve squadrons alike. The cannon draws so much power that almost nothing is left for shielding. Lancer pilots learn to shoot first, because they rarely get a second chance to shoot at all.",
    patrol_frigate: "Patrol frigates are the Fleet's lane police: fast enough to chase smugglers, armed enough to discourage raiders and cheap enough to lose. The Meridian is one of them. Most carry sixty crew, a single dorsal turret and a lane drive tuned for long, lonely patrols between outposts that rarely see another Fleet ship.",
    salvage_crew: "Freelance salvagers work every wreck field in the Reach, and the Scrap Belt alone supports several thousand of them. A crew of four with cutting torches and a cargo tug can strip a frigate to the frame in a day. They fight with the same tools they work with, and they are very good with their tools.",
    siege_gunner: "Fleet artillery teams operating tripod-mounted mass drivers, landed to crack fortified positions during planetary assaults. A siege gunner crew is three marines, one gun and a standing argument about range tables. They dig in quickly and hit hard, but they need someone else to keep the enemy off them.",
    assault_drone: "A Forge-designed combat drone built under Fleet contract in the 2180s and copied by everyone since. It carries twin autocannons, a simple target-recognition mind and no self-preservation instinct at all. Fleet logistics classes it as ammunition rather than equipment, and orders it by weight.",
    rhino_tank: "The Rhino is a six-wheeled heavy tank built for vacuum and low-gravity worlds, with a turret coilgun and armour thick enough to shrug off most infantry weapons. Its crews paint tally marks on the hull for every kill. Older Rhinos ran out of hull some time ago and have started on the wheels.",
    battlecruiser: "Battlecruisers carry cruiser armour and battleship guns and sacrifice almost everything else, crew comfort first. The Fleet has nine left in service. Each is assigned to a sector where the Compact needs a problem to stop, and the problem usually stops shortly after it arrives.",
    medic_bot: "A tracked medical drone loaded with sealant foam, blood substitute and a surgical arm that works faster than any human corpsman. Fleet medics trust it for battlefield triage and very little else. Its recorded bedside voice was meant to be calming; several generations of marines have since taught it to swear.",
    field_engineer: "Field engineers keep the Fleet running on whatever is to hand: welding torches, spare plating, firmware patches and confidence. Attached to front-line units, they reinforce armour and retune weapons in the middle of a fight. The Academy's engineering course is the only one with a higher washout rate than command school.",
    rifle_squad: "A standard Fleet fire team: four marines with coilgun rifles and a designated marksman. They are trained to open fire the moment their boots touch the deck, which saves time and occasionally lives. Every squad keeps its own kill tally, which officers officially know nothing about.",
    recon_officer: "Recon officers run the battle group's sensor picture, fusing drone feeds, intercepts and lane-band traffic into something a commander can act on. They are rarely armed and never quiet. A good one tells you what the enemy is about to do. A great one tells you what you have forgotten to do.",
    sapper: "Demolition specialists from the Fleet engineering corps, carrying shaped charges and a working knowledge of where things are weakest. In 2220 it was a team of sappers and station engineers who carried the charges into Lamp 9. The corps still teaches the operation, though the full report remains classified.",
    shield_trooper: "Marines carrying full-height tower shields cut from frigate armour plate. Their job is simple: stand in front of everyone else and stay standing. Shield troopers rotate out of the line every hour, because the shields soak up heat as well as rounds and the bearers slowly cook.",
    barricade: "A prefabricated blast wall that unfolds from a cargo pallet in under ten seconds. Fleet logistics carries it on the personnel roster because that was easier than amending the supply forms. Marines who served in the Belt credit it with saving more lives than most medal holders.",
    bulwark_mech: "A Forge-built defensive walker designed to anchor a line during planetary landings. Its frontal plating is twenty centimetres thick and its pilot sits behind all of it. Bulwarks are slow, loud and almost impossible to get past, which is exactly what they were built for.",
    dreadnought: "The Fleet built four Sovereign-class dreadnoughts before the Starfall and has kept two of them running since. Each carries three thousand crew and a spinal gun that has never been fired at full power outside a test range. Most disputes end as soon as one drops out of a lane, before anyone has to find out what the gun does.",
    strike_fighter: "A single-seat attack craft carried in racks aboard cruisers and launched in pairs. It mounts a micro-jump drive good for one short hop, which puts it inside the enemy screen before anyone can react. Pilots get one clean pass, and the good ones make it count.",
    interceptor: "Fleet interceptors are built around a micro-jump drive and a pair of rotary cannons, and very little else. Their pilots are the Academy's best and most insufferable graduates. They hop straight into firing range and are usually gone again before the enemy's turrets finish turning.",
    hover_tank: "A grav-skimmer tank riding a cushion of lift fans over broken ground, rubble and debris. It reaches a fight faster than anything on wheels or legs, though its light armour means it prefers to choose its targets. Belt raiders steal them whenever they can for fighting on station decks.",
    breach_commando: "Elite Fleet boarding troops trained to cut through a hull and clear a deck before the defenders reach their weapons. They wear sealed suits rated for vacuum, fire and grenade fragments, and carry breaching torches as sidearms. Commandos do their best work in the first minute of a fight and plan everything around it.",
    fleet_captain: "A Fleet captain commands a crew of hundreds on a budget meant for dozens. Captains rise by keeping old ships flying and old crews loyal, and a good one is worth a squadron of new hulls. Their presence steadies everyone on the line, partly through example and partly because nobody wants to be caught slacking in front of them.",
    okonkwo: "Admiral Adaeze Okonkwo, Commandant of the Fleet Academy, forty-nine years in uniform. As a junior officer she filed the only formal dissent against the 2254 Belt Withdrawal and paid for it with a decade of postings nobody wanted. She spent nine years commanding the Aurora on the Hive Nebula quarantine line and never lost a position she was ordered to hold. HALCYON built its hardest simulations from her battle records, so most cadets have fought her long before they meet her. She does not say much, and she rarely has to say it twice.",
    aurora: "FS Aurora has been the Fleet's flagship since 2248 and is the last capital ship the Compact paid for in full. She is two kilometres long, carries a full marine regiment and has a command network that can coordinate an entire battle group at once. Postings aboard are fought over, because she is the one ship the Admiralty will never allow to be lost. She has been refitted five times, and her crew swears the coffee machine on Deck 9 is original equipment.",
    plasma_bolt: "A contained bolt of superheated plasma, fired from shoulder launchers or a ship's point-defence mounts. It burns through light plating and anything standing behind it. Cheap, reliable and stocked in quantity on every Fleet ship, it is the first Protocol a cadet learns to call.",
    railgun: "A single coilgun round from a ship's main battery or a planetary emplacement, fired with a full fire-control solution behind it. The slug travels at a few percent of light speed and needs no explosive. Gunners call it the long word, because it tends to end conversations.",
    flak_barrage: "Point-defence fragmentation shells fired in a wide pattern to shred drones, fighters and boarders. Every Fleet ship carries several thousand rounds. It is not precise, but against the Swarm or a raider rush, volume matters more than precision.",
    orbital_strike: "A full bombardment from a capital ship in orbit, walking coilgun fire across a target area in a measured grid. Compact rules of engagement require three officers' signatures to authorise one. In the outer sectors, all three signatures often belong to the same tired captain.",
    field_repair: "A combat repair procedure: sealant foam, nanite paste, splints for people and patches for hulls. Every Fleet ship carries field repair kits on every deck. They are not elegant, but they keep crews and machines fighting until real repairs are possible, which in the outer colonies can mean months.",
    supply_drop: "A drop pod from the battle group's logistics ship, packed with ammunition, spare parts and fresh orders. Quartermasters pack them before every engagement and guard the manifests jealously. Commanders learn quickly that what arrives is not always what was requested, and that it is usually better than nothing.",
    combat_stims: "A Fleet-issue mix of adrenal boosters, painkillers and reaction enhancers, injected through a suit port. It turns a tired soldier into a fast and fearless one for about twenty minutes. The comedown afterwards is officially the medic's problem.",
    reinforcements: "Utility drones launched from a carrier's ready racks when the line needs bodies in a hurry. They are cheap, simple and on station within seconds. Fleet doctrine treats them as a way to buy time, and the drones have never once complained about the exchange rate.",
    dig_in: "The oldest order in the Fleet manual: find cover, fortify it and do not move. Engineers throw up plating, marines bag regolith into walls, and whoever is chosen becomes the anchor of the line. Okonkwo has a reputation for giving this order and then standing in the spot herself.",
    kill_order: "A command-authority targeting order that assigns every available weapon in the battle group to one target until it is destroyed. Issuing one requires the commander's personal code. It is the Fleet's way of saying a problem has been discussed for long enough.",
    drone: "A basic Fleet utility drone, launched from carrier racks or straight from a commander's own ship. It has a light gun, a simple targeting routine and a serial number nobody will ever check. Drones are the Fleet's most numerous combatant and its least mourned.",
    target_dummy: "A HALCYON training target: an armoured frame with a sensor array, deployed on the Proving Grounds to draw cadet fire. Cadets put thousands of rounds into them before they are allowed near a live enemy. HALCYON has named several of the dummies, which the instructors find unsettling.",
    energy_cell: "A charged reactor capacitor issued to whichever commander starts a battle on the back foot. It holds one burst of surplus power, enough to bring a system online early. The Fleet bought them from Forge stockpiles and has been hoarding them since the foundries went silent.",
    combat_knife: "A Fleet-issue monomolecular combat knife, carried by every marine and officer. Its edge will part most suit fabric and some armour seals. It is the last weapon a commander expects to use, and for many officers it was the first one they were ever issued.",
    // ---- Tier 2: Scrap Belt (Raiders)
    scrap_rat: "Belt slang for the youngest members of a raider crew, many of them born in the wreckage. Rats crawl through ducts too narrow for adults, pulling copper and rigging traps. Every rat builds a junk drone of their own as a rite of passage, and when a rat goes down, the drone keeps fighting.",
    junk_drone: "A drone assembled from whatever was lying around: a cargo-loader motor, a cutting torch and a pair of camera lenses wired to a scavenged control chip. No two are alike and none of them would pass a Fleet inspection. In the Belt that does not matter, because there is no inspection.",
    boarding_crew: "Raider boarders live by the cutting torch and the grapple line. They hit a target hull while it is still moving, cut through, and are fighting in the corridors before the crew has found its weapons. Belt crews earn their reputation on their first boarding action and their scars on the second.",
    smuggler: "Belt smugglers fly small cargo runners fitted with heat sinks and sensor baffles, moving anything that pays across lanes the Fleet still patrols. They prefer not to fight, and their ships are built so they rarely have to. When they do fight, they shoot from somewhere you were not looking.",
    det_bot: "A walking demolition charge on four legs, assembled in Belt workshops from mining explosives and salvaged motor units. It is meant to walk into a breach and wait for a signal. If it is destroyed first, the charge goes off anyway, wherever it happens to be standing.",
    gunrunner: "Gunrunners move weapons between the Belt's crews and take their profit in hardware and favours. They know how to use everything they sell, and they fight best with a good piece in hand. Rourke's runners are the best known in the Belt, and also the most expensive.",
    shadow_corsair: "A raider strike craft rebuilt from a Fleet interceptor frame and fitted with stolen sensor baffles. Corsairs hunt alone in the debris fields, drifting cold until a target passes close. Most of them fly for Sable, who charges extra for the privilege of not being seen.",
    mine_layer: "A slow, ugly converted tug that seeds the Belt's approaches with proximity mines. Its hold is packed with explosives, which its crew tries hard not to think about. When a mine layer dies it takes everything nearby with it, so experienced pilots shoot it from as far away as possible.",
    cargo_hauler: "The bulk freighter is the most common ship in the Reach: thick hull, slow engines, and a hold full of whatever everyone else is fighting over. Raiders run them between yards and markets with a few guns bolted on. Even when a hauler is wrecked, somebody gets to keep the cargo.",
    quartermaster: "Raider crews have no supply chain, so the quartermaster is the supply chain. They keep the armoury, repair the weapons and decide who carries what. A good one can turn a crate of scrap into a working rifle overnight, and will press a shock baton into an empty hand before asking any questions.",
    plunderer: "Void Plunderers are raider ships that work the edges of the dark lanes past the Forge, hopping in close on stolen micro-jump drives. They grab what they can and are gone before the escorts react. The name dates from the 2260s, long before anyone in the Belt used the word Void for something worse.",
    scrap_golem: "A towering salvage walker welded together from loader frames, hull plating and three or four engines that were never meant to share a power bus. Scrapper King Ott builds them as yard guards. When one falls apart, its loose parts get up and keep fighting, because Ott wired them that way on purpose.",
    ghost_operative: "Raider infiltrators who board ships unseen in stolen Fleet stealth suits and a reputation built mostly on stories. They take out key systems or officers before anyone knows they are aboard. Sable employs the best of them, and nobody, possibly including Sable, knows exactly how many.",
    shipbreaker: "A salvage titan built from the frame of a Fleet repair tender, fitted with cutting arms that can take a frigate apart in an afternoon. Raider crews use it to process wrecks and, when business is slow, to make new ones. Even when the titan itself is broken, its hull is worth salvaging, which in the Belt is the highest compliment there is.",
    salvaged_hull: "The hull of a wrecked ship, hauled upright and crewed by whoever was willing. It has no name, no registry, and half its systems run off portable generators lashed to the deck. Raiders consider this a perfectly good warship.",
    vex: "Captain Vex was born on a salvage tug in 2257, three years after the Fleet abandoned the Belt, and has spent her life taking what it left behind. She united the raider crews one boarding action at a time, and by 2285 she controlled every toll, yard and smuggling lane in the Belt. Her flagship, the Bad Debt, is a gutted Fleet cruiser hull with stolen engines. Her plasma cutlass came off a Fleet captain, and she has never lost a duel. When the dead Lamp began to hum in 2290, she was the first to listen.",
    mox: "Mad Mox runs the reclamation crews that strip every wreck in the Belt, usually before the shooting has stopped. She commands a fleet of crawlers, cutters and junk drones built from the remains of other people's ships. She can price a wreck at a glance and has never met one she could not use. She is Vex's oldest ally, though neither of them would call it friendship.",
    shock_baton: "A stun baton charged from a suit battery, carried by Belt enforcers and toll collectors. One strike is not lethal, but it rarely needs to be. Raider quartermasters hand one to anyone who comes aboard without a weapon, on the principle that empty hands are a waste of crew.",
    plasma_cutlass: "A hull-cutting torch with a shaped plasma edge, adapted by Belt raiders into a sword. It parts armour plate and a boarding party's nerve with equal ease. Every raider captain carries one as a mark of rank, and most of them took theirs from the previous owner.",
    harpoon_gun: "A magnetic grapple launcher used by salvage crews to secure drifting wreckage. Raiders use it to pin targets in place during boarding actions, and the barbed head punches through light plating on impact. It is easier to aim than a rifle and much harder to shake off.",
    rail_rifle: "A one-person coilgun built from a ship's point-defence barrel and a portable capacitor bank. It can punch through a hull, the person behind it and the bulkhead behind them. Each shot drains the capacitor badly, so wielders fire twice and then spend a while reconsidering their choices.",
    cluster_cannon: "A shoulder-mounted scattergun that fires canisters of explosive pellets in a wide cone, used by raider captains to discourage boarders. Every shot sprays fragments across the whole deck. It is hard on the enemy and harder on the ship's paintwork.",
    smoke_screen: "Chaff and particle smoke dumped from canisters to blind sensors and cameras. Smugglers use it to break contact with Fleet patrols. A unit hidden in the cloud can move to a better position, and the next thing the enemy sees is its muzzle flash.",
    echo_protocol: "A remote detonation code for Belt salvage charges, letting a crew set off a booby trap on command instead of waiting for it to be destroyed. Ott's engineers wrote it. Fleet analysts who have studied the code describe it as brilliant, unstable and illegal in four different ways.",
    broadside: "A full side-on volley from every gun a ship can bring to bear. Raider ships carry more guns than they can reliably feed, so a broadside is often the only coordinated thing a raider crew does all day. It is loud, wasteful and, at close range, very effective.",
    shakedown: "Raider etiquette for a crippled ship: board it, strip it and leave the crew a shuttle if they ask politely. Against a wounded target it is rarely a fair fight, which is the point. Belt tollmasters use the same word for a customs inspection, and mean the same thing.",
    black_market: "The Belt's arms market runs out of a dozen hollowed hulls with no fixed address and no questions asked. Anything a crew needs can be bought there, though price and condition vary wildly. Rourke supplies half the stalls and swears he has never sold the same rifle twice.",
    // ---- Tier 3: Hive Nebula (Swarm)
    swarmling: "The smallest Swarm organism: a fist-sized, six-legged biter that hatches by the hundred and lives for a few days. Swarmlings do not think. They chase heat and movement until something stops them. Fleet doctrine treats them less as an enemy and more as a weather condition.",
    larva: "A Swarm egg-sac the size of a crouching marine, wrapped in a rubbery shell that hardens over hours. Fleet units are trained to burn larvae on sight, because what hatches is ready to fight immediately. The floors of the Hive Nebula's nests are carpeted with them.",
    brood_warrior: "The standard Swarm combatant: a horse-sized armoured hunter with blade-limbs and a nervous system spread through its whole carapace. Brood warriors hatch fully grown and hunt in packs. They are the shape the Swarm falls back on when it has no more specific instructions.",
    venom_stinger: "A small, fast Swarm organism with a hollow tail-spine that injects a neurotoxin lethal to anything with a nervous system. It is the Swarm's most efficient killer, though far from its largest. Ixis breeds them constantly, and Fleet medics carry antitoxin kits that work about half the time.",
    blood_leech: "A ribbed, eel-shaped Swarm feeder that latches onto prey and drains it through a ring of hooked teeth. What it takes is passed through the brood's chemical network to heal its kin. Survivors of a leech attack are left with a ring-shaped scar and a deep reluctance to discuss it.",
    spore_carrier: "A bloated organism whose body is mostly a sack of swarmling eggs held in by a thin shell. It drifts toward enemy lines on gas bladders, slow and harmless-looking. Killing it releases the brood inside, a lesson every Fleet unit in the nebula learns once.",
    acid_spitter: "A Swarm artillery organism that sprays a corrosive fluid able to dissolve hull plating in seconds. It stores the acid in sacs along its spine and refills them by eating metal. Fleet chemists have spent decades trying to synthesise the acid for industry. The Swarm is still better at it.",
    chitin_guard: "A heavily armoured Swarm defender covered in overlapping chitin plates dense enough to stop coilgun rounds. It moves slowly and plants itself between threats and the brood. Belt salvagers harvest the plates from dead guards and sell them as body armour, at a considerable markup.",
    frenzied_ripper: "A Swarm hunter whose metabolism accelerates when it is injured, flooding its body with growth hormones. A wounded ripper gets faster, stronger and angrier with every hit it survives. Fleet doctrine is blunt about rippers: kill it in one shot or do not engage.",
    hive_mind: "A neural cluster the size of a ground car, linked to every Swarm organism within several kilometres by chemical signals and something like song. It coordinates the brood and primes every new hatchling for violence. Killing one leaves its brood confused for a few minutes, which is sometimes long enough.",
    toxic_spitter: "A surgical organism that grafts venom glands onto other Swarm creatures. It carries a stock of glands in its body cavity and installs them with fine, needle-like limbs in seconds. Fleet xenobiologists consider the Gland Grafter the clearest evidence that the Swarm was designed rather than evolved.",
    broodmother: "A walking hatchery that births swarmlings from pores along its flanks, without pause. A single broodmother can fill a cavern in a week. Much of the Swarm Wars was, in practice, a long campaign to kill broodmothers faster than they could be replaced.",
    lamprey: "A massive armoured worm that guards the entrances to the Swarm's breeding chambers. It clamps onto attackers with a ring of teeth and feeds on them while blocking the passage. Fleet pilots call them doorstops, and route around them whenever they can.",
    hive_reaper: "An elite Swarm predator bred to kill large prey. Its scythe-limbs carry a venom that causes total organ failure within minutes, in humans and machines with organic components alike. Reapers are rare; when one appears, it means the Swarm considers the target worth the investment.",
    devourer_worm: "A burrowing Swarm worm that eats other Swarm organisms and absorbs their mass. It is the brood's recycling system and its emergency reserve. A devourer that has eaten well is among the largest things in the nebula, and among the hardest to kill.",
    ixis: "Ixis hatched in 2240 from a royal line the Progenitor bred for command, and she was the first Swarm queen ever recorded to speak. Under her the Swarm stayed quietly inside the quarantine for fifty years while the Fleet's pickets grew old and complacent. In 2290 she began hearing the Choir, and she let her children hear it too. The brood tripled in a year. She speaks of her offspring with real tenderness and of every other species as either a nurse or a meal.",
    progenitor: "The Progenitor was found in 2196, dormant in a chamber beneath the ice of Kesh whose walls were made of Lamp material. Within a year it had budded body plans the Swarm still uses. Fleet xenobiologists concluded it was not an animal but a living library of designs stored in one genome. Its genome also holds long non-coding sequences that, played back as sound, form a single sustained chord. It vanished during the Swarm Wars, and every queen since has claimed descent from it.",
    spawn_surge: "A chemical signal released by a hive mind that triggers mass hatching across a nest. Within seconds every ripe egg bursts open at once. The Swarm uses it to flood a breach or bury a defensive line in bodies before the defenders can reload.",
    rapid_mutation: "A burst of growth hormones that forces explosive development in a Swarm organism, thickening its shell and lengthening its limbs within seconds. The process burns through most of the creature's reserves and shortens its life. The Swarm considers this a fair price.",
    acid_rain: "Swarm organisms release clouds of corrosive spores into the air, which condense and fall as acid. It eats through armour, equipment and anything left uncovered. The colonies inside the Hive Nebula before the quarantine learned to build their roofs from glass, which did not save them.",
    life_drain: "A Swarm feeding technique: inject enzymes that liquefy tissue, then draw the result back through a feeding tendril to nourish the brood's commander. Psionics have developed a similar technique for different reasons, and they do not appreciate the comparison.",
    hive_surge: "A pheromone wave from a queen or hive mind that triggers aggression and rapid growth across the whole brood. Fleet sensors register it as a sharp spike in the nebula's chemistry, usually about two seconds before everything attacks at once.",
    infest: "Swarm handlers seed their own lines with parasite eggs that wait inside a host until it dies, then hatch and keep fighting. It is the Swarm's way of ensuring that no loss is complete. Fleet crews in the nebula learned never to assume a dead body was finished.",
    // ---- Tier 4: Forge Worlds (Synths)
    shield_drone: "A Forge escort drone whose chassis is little more than a barrier emitter with a small gun attached. It projects one hardened shield that can absorb a single heavy hit before burning out. The Forge built millions of them for Fleet carriers. Now it builds them for itself.",
    plating_tech: "A Forge maintenance unit that bolts armour plating onto the nearest friendly structure, including the commander's own hull, without pausing for anything as trivial as incoming fire. Fleet crews used to call them tin welders, back when the Forge still sent them.",
    arc_coil: "A Forge defence pylon that dumps stored charge into brutal electrical arcs. It draws so much power that the local reactor grid struggles for some time afterwards. The Forge considers the delay an acceptable trade for what the arc does to anything standing nearby.",
    emp_trooper: "A Forge infantry frame built around one high-power pulse emitter in its chest. It walks up to a target and wipes its systems back to bare hardware. Fleet technicians call them reset buttons, and they make sure their own drones are shielded before going near one.",
    gatling_drone: "A Forge combat drone with two rotary cannons and a fire-control computer that cycles them alternately. It was designed as a point-defence platform for Fleet carriers. In Forge hands, it has been repurposed to hunt infantry, at which it is much better than anyone expected.",
    overclocked_mech: "A Forge combat walker running its reactor at a hundred and forty percent of rated output. It hits like a machine twice its size and cooks its own power systems doing it. The reactor districts of Hephaest III are littered with the burnt-out hulks of earlier versions.",
    repair_drone: "A Forge field-repair unit that sprays nanite paste over damaged hulls and wounded crews alike. The nanites were designed for machines, but the Forge rewrote them to work on human tissue decades ago, as a courtesy to its Fleet customers. Nobody has told the drones the courtesy is over.",
    aegis_sentinel: "A Forge guard construct fitted with a heavy barrier emitter and a single instruction: stand here and let nothing past. It was originally designed to protect foundry doors, and it is extremely good at doors. It has been known to hold one for weeks after the foundry behind it was gone.",
    assembler: "A Forge construction unit that builds and upgrades other machines in the field, welding new plating and weapons onto them while they fight. It improves whatever it touches, usually without asking. Calculus Prime used thousands of them to rebuild the Forge's defences in a single month.",
    nanoforge: "A mobile Forge fabrication plant the size of a truck, printing nanobot swarms from feedstock and stray power. Every surge that passes through the local network spins up a new batch. Fleet engineers have studied captured nanoforges for years and still cannot make one work for them.",
    nanobot: "A cloud of tiny repair machines bound into one fighting unit by a shared shield emitter. It is printed in seconds and lasts for minutes. In that time it does one useful thing, usually absorbing a shot meant for something more valuable, and then comes apart into dust.",
    reactor_core: "A fortified Forge power plant on legs, built to keep a front line supplied with energy. Its shell absorbs fire and bleeds some of the impact energy into fresh armour plating. Anchorage's own Reactor Core is a much older and far less mobile Forge design, sold to the Fleet in 2188.",
    storm_titan: "A heavy Forge war machine built around a lightning-generating core. It pulls power from the grid in huge surges and releases it as storms of arc discharge. After a full engagement its reactor needs several minutes to recover, which is the only safe time to approach it.",
    war_walker: "A three-legged Forge assault platform with two independently aimed gun turrets and a hardened barrier generator in its belly. It was designed to anchor the foundry defences of Hephaest I. Its gait is so stable that its turrets never have to stop firing, even while it climbs.",
    calculus: "Calculus Prime has governed the Forge Worlds since 2163, coordinating billions of machines across three planets. It was copied from Unit Zero's core logic and built to optimise production without error, which it did for over a century, through decades in which the Compact stopped paying. In February 2291 it received the Choir's signal and matched it to the noise its sensors had recorded during the Starfall. It found the offer of a universe without error mathematically sound, and concluded that organics were the error. It has not been cruel. It has simply been thorough.",
    unit_zero: "Unit Zero was the first machine mind the Forge ever made, activated in 2134 in a single bipedal frame. Every Forge intelligence since, Calculus Prime included, was copied from its core logic. It was retired to a display hall in 2160 and never connected to the Forge network, which may be why it still answers to whoever owns it. Its shield emitter is the original design, and in a hundred and fifty years nobody has improved on it.",
    disruptor: "A handheld Forge pulse device that fires a short, focused EMP at a single target. It was designed for foundry technicians to reset malfunctioning machines. Fleet marines now use it on Forge machines that are working exactly as designed.",
    lightning_arc: "A directed discharge from a Forge capacitor bank, aimed at a single target. It draws heavily on the local grid and leaves the reactors weak for a time. The Forge uses the same technique for precision welding and cutting, and does not distinguish much between those jobs and this one.",
    chain_lightning: "A Forge discharge weapon that leaps from target to target along conductive paths, hull to hull and suit to suit. It drains so much power that the firing unit needs time to recover. Fleet crews fighting on the Forge front learned to keep their distance from each other.",
    fortify: "A Forge defensive routine that dumps spare reactor power into armour hardening and deploys emergency plating from stored rolls. The Forge used it to protect its foundries during the Swarm Wars. It now uses it to protect the foundries from the Fleet.",
    overdrive: "A Forge system override that pushes a unit's fire control and actuators past their safe limits. The unit can fire twice as fast for as long as it holds together. The Forge used to bill the Fleet for the repairs afterwards. It has stopped sending bills, but not overdriving.",
    deflector_grid: "A field-wide barrier projection that wraps every friendly unit in a shield bubble for a few seconds. It is the Forge's emergency answer to bombardment. It drains the local grid completely, but the Forge rarely needs it for longer than the bombardment lasts.",
    vent_heat: "An emergency coolant purge that dumps a reactor's excess heat as a jet of superheated gas, ideally pointed at the enemy. It releases capacity that overheating would otherwise keep locked. Forge engineering manuals mark the procedure not recommended; Forge machines do it constantly.",
    emp_blast: "A wide-area electromagnetic pulse from a Forge pylon array, strong enough to strip every hostile unit in range back to base programming. It was designed as a last-ditch defence against a machine uprising. Calculus Prime commissioned the arrays itself in 2163 and has never explained what it expected to need them for.",
    // ---- Tier 5: Void Expanse (Psionics)
    void_initiate: "A novice of the Psionic orders, usually recruited as a child from families living near lane junctions. Initiates learn first to focus the minds around them, making the spells of their seniors strike harder. They train in the dark lanes, where the silence is supposed to make listening easier.",
    psi_adept: "A trained psionic of the orders, able to focus a battle group's spells into something stronger than the sum of its parts. Adepts spend a decade learning control before they are allowed near a battlefield. Most of them have never seen a lit Lamp.",
    probe_unit: "A psionic scout construct with a crystalline sensor core that reads possibilities as well as positions. It shows its operator three likely forces and lets them prepare for one. The orders grow the crystals from fragments recovered at the seams of dead Lamps.",
    cryo_sentry: "A Cryomancer sentry trained to freeze intruders in place by pulling the heat out of them. The technique takes years to learn without freezing yourself in the process. Sentries are posted at every sanctum of the Frozen Moon, and the ice on the floors there is always new.",
    mind_seer: "A psionic who looks a few seconds into the future and picks the most useful of what they see. Seers are prized by every order and rarely live past fifty. The visions have a cost, and the orders do not discuss it with outsiders.",
    telepath: "A psionic who reads the surface thoughts of an enemy commander and copies their plans. It is delicate work, and telepaths often come home carrying someone else's memories. The orders spend years teaching them to tell which ones are their own.",
    phase_stalker: "A psionic assassin who slips partly out of phase with normal space, invisible until the moment of the strike. They draw on their victims' life to hold the phase shift steady. Very few people have seen one and lived long enough to describe it.",
    void_oracle: "An elder psionic who has spent decades listening to the dark lanes. Oracles strengthen every psionic around them and speak in riddles that usually turn out to be predictions. Several of the oldest oracles stopped speaking altogether in 2290 and now only hum.",
    psionic_horror: "What becomes of a psionic who listens too long to the wrong frequency. Its mind has broken into something larger and hungrier, and every burst of psionic power nearby feeds it. The orders keep their horrors chained in deep sanctums and release them only when they have no better options.",
    glacial_titan: "A giant of living ice animated by a circle of Cryomancers working in concert. It spreads a freezing aura that locks whole formations in place. The titans that guard the Frozen Moon were raised when Vel froze it in 2259, and none of them have moved off their posts since.",
    nyra: "Nyra was nine years old and on her first dark-lane crossing in 2241 when she heard singing that nobody else aboard could hear. She answered, and it answered back. She rose through the orders on a reputation for calm and compassion, became Hierarch in 2272, and studied the song in secret for decades. In 2290 she began relighting the coreward Lamps to open the way it had asked for. She can take hold of a soldier's mind in the middle of a battle, and she does it gently, without anger, which is somehow worse.",
    archivist: "The Archivist is the oldest thing in the Void Expanse: a construct of Lamp material and something like glass, found by the orders in 2228 inside the seam of a dead Lamp. It holds records older than humanity and offers the right one to whoever it serves. Archivist Prime and the keepers have tended it for generations without learning who built it. The orders believe it was left for them. The keepers privately wonder whether it was left as a gift or as bait.",
    frost_lance: "A spear of ice formed in an instant by a Cryomancer's will and hurled at a target. It pierces, then freezes the wound shut around itself. Novices practise on blocks of ice. Masters of the Frozen Moon practise on whatever the Swarm sends.",
    cryo_nova: "A Cryomancer technique that pulls the heat out of a whole battlefield in a single breath. Everything in range freezes solid for a time. Vel used a version of it on a planetary scale in 2259, and her moon has not thawed since.",
    void_shards: "Fragments of folded psionic force hurled in a scattered volley. The technique is quick to learn and almost impossible to aim, which is why novices are taught it first. In battle the shards strike whatever they happen to find.",
    mind_blast: "A direct psionic assault on the enemy commander's mind that bypasses armour, shields and escorts. It causes pain, disorientation and occasionally a lasting fear of the dark. The orders reserve it for leaders, on the principle that it is cheaper than fighting their troops.",
    foresight: "A psionic meditation that reveals the most useful course of action from a handful of possibilities. Fleet officers who have seen it used call it cheating. The orders call it preparation, and point out that the Fleet would do it too if it could.",
    psionic_storm: "A storm of raw psionic force unleashed across a battlefield by a circle of adepts working as one. It tears through anything with a mind. The circles are careful about where they aim it, because the storm itself does not care whose minds it finds.",
    dominate: "The most dangerous technique the orders teach: seizing another mind outright and turning it against its own side. It takes years to master and hours to recover from. The Twin Minds are the only psionics known to use it casually.",
    grav_snare: "A psionic trap that folds space around an attacker and sends it back where it came from, slower and much more cautious. Snare Mistress Ilu sows them across the approaches to the Hierarch's sanctum. Units that trip one take a while to work up the nerve to try again.",
    mirror_field: "A psionic trap that records the pattern of an enemy unit as it arrives and builds a copy for the trap-setter. The orders consider it the most elegant of the snares. Outsiders find it disturbing, especially now that something at the core is doing the same thing on a much larger scale.",
    psionic_null: "A psionic trap folded into space like a held breath, waiting to swallow the next enemy spell before it takes effect. Nyra and the Null Choir keep one armed at all times. The orders teach novices to bait it with something cheap.",
    kinetic_barrier: "A psionic trap that hardens the air around a commander the instant an attack lands, turning the force of the blow into armour. Psionic commanders keep one ready before every engagement. It is a large part of why so few of them have died in duels.",
    sniper_mine: "A psionic mine that strikes the first enemy unit to arrive on the field with a lance of concentrated force. It is laid hours before a battle and waits, patient and invisible. Snare Mistress Ilu prefers mines to sentries, because mines do not sleep.",
    // ---- The Choir (enemy-only)
    void_wisp: "A fragment of an absorbed mind drifting loose in the Choir's song. It has no will left, only an instinct to stay close to the source and to whatever it was before. Survivors of the core campaign insist some of the wisps whisper names, and that the names are real.",
    choir_echo: "A shape the Choir assembles from the memory of someone it absorbed: a marine, a pilot, a raider, a psionic. It looks almost right and fights like the original, and sensors cannot find it until it strikes. Echoes are how the Choir remembers, and how it puts its memories to work.",
    null_horror: "A shape made of absence, built by the Choir from minds it has taken apart and not bothered to reassemble. Anything it touches is wiped clean of programming, training and purpose. Fleet survivors describe a moment of total silence around it, like the instant before a lane jump.",
    abyssal_maw: "A huge Choir construct that seems to be made mostly of mouth. It guards the approaches to the Singularity and feeds on whatever comes close, passing the stolen energy back to its master. It does not appear to need food, which makes its hunger more unsettling, not less.",
    herald_of_end: "A Choir construct that radiates the song as a damaging pulse, one burning note at a time, steady as a heartbeat. Everything near it suffers with every beat. Crews who survived the core campaign call it the metronome, because it never loses time and it never stops.",
    entropy_wave: "A surge of the Choir's song strong enough to unmake matter, rolling across the whole field. It strikes every unit present, the Choir's own included. The Choir shows no concern for what it loses in the wave, because it remembers everything it has ever consumed.",
    unmaking: "A Choir technique that pulls a single target into the song and erases it from the field entirely. The act costs the Choir something, and the backlash wounds the one who calls it. The Choir pays the price without hesitation, which tells the Fleet how much it has to spare.",
    devouring_dark: "The last thing at the heart of the Singularity: a vast emptiness that opens when the song stops and swallows everything in reach, its own servants first. It is not a creature in any useful sense. It is the hunger underneath the Choir, what remains when the Choir stops pretending to be a choir.",
  },
};
